import "core"


# DISABLED — a compiler gap, kept as the repro of it.
  A list literal of two or more elements fails to typecheck when it is written into a field of a
  data type, though the same literal is fine everywhere else it has been tried.
  What works, each checked on its own:
    - @["x", "y"].length@ written directly — no field, no data type.
    - @howMany(tags= ["x", "y"])@ where @howMany(tags -> /Data/List[$T -> String])@ — the same
      element bound as the field below carries, reached as an argument rather than as a field.
    - @/Tagged(name= "a", tags= [])@ and @/Tagged(name= "a", tags= ["x"])@ — the field itself, with
      nothing in it and with one thing in it.
  What fails is only the field holding two or more, and it fails for two of the same string as
  readily as for two different ones, so it is the second [/Data/Cons] rather than the two elements
  disagreeing:
    Scheme failed verification verifySchemeActLowers
    Grows by: (fromList [],∅)
    ... at setScheme {Tuple Meta In /Data/Cons(Value /head= CStr "x")(EAppArg Value /tail= Value
    /Data/Nil) :: ACT /Data/Cons[/T=UnionType(Nothing, ∅, [CStr "x"])](/head=UnionType(Nothing, ∅,
    [CStr "x"]), /tail=/Data/Nil); REQ /Data/Cons[/T=$_/T](/head=$/T, /tail=/Data/Nil)}
  The [/T] of the INNER cons is the OUTER head's literal rather than the [/Data/Primitive/String]
  the field was declared to hold — so the bound on the field is not reaching the nested cons, and
  what reaches it instead is the element type worked out one level up.
data(Tagged(name -> String, tags -> /Data/List[$T -> String]))

every(Tagged.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

/Tagged(name= "two of the same", tags= ["x", "x"])
/Tagged(name= "two different", tags= ["x", "y"])

main{io -> IO}= io.exit(val= 0)
